WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.167
(beeping)

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.340
- Check the sonar.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:05.411
Do we have a lock on it?

00:00:05.411 --> 00:00:06.358
- 50 feet, captain.

00:00:06.358 --> 00:00:08.251
- I'm reading her to be
at least 28 feet long.

00:00:08.251 --> 00:00:09.192
- 28 feet?

00:00:09.192 --> 00:00:10.192
Great Scott!

00:00:14.152 --> 00:00:17.079
Legends have told of a giant sea serpent.

00:00:17.079 --> 00:00:17.912
- There!

00:00:26.238 --> 00:00:29.541
- The inspiration for legends
of sea serpents and monsters,

00:00:29.541 --> 00:00:33.272
the giant oarfish is a peculiar animal.

00:00:33.272 --> 00:00:35.821
It's the world's longest bony fish,

00:00:35.821 --> 00:00:37.208
and has been known to reach lengths

00:00:37.208 --> 00:00:38.625
of up to 56 feet.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.552
Of those we have been able to study,

00:00:41.552 --> 00:00:45.552
they typically weigh in
at around 600 pounds.

00:00:45.552 --> 00:00:48.780
Oarfish can be found in
every ocean on the planet,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.251
except the Arctic.

00:00:50.251 --> 00:00:53.587
They're what's known as
an oceanodromous species.

00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:54.420
- What?

00:00:56.721 --> 00:00:58.661
- It means the fish is migratory,

00:00:58.661 --> 00:01:02.329
and travels across the world's oceans.

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:03.849
- Exactly.

00:01:03.849 --> 00:01:06.158
Oarfish migrate to find their food,

00:01:06.158 --> 00:01:08.758
mainly plankton and krill.

00:01:08.758 --> 00:01:10.429
While the fish may be large,

00:01:10.429 --> 00:01:13.751
it has no real teeth,
but rather gill rakers

00:01:13.751 --> 00:01:17.501
that filter out its food
from the ocean water.

00:01:21.463 --> 00:01:25.142
The giant oarfish is
typically a deep ocean fish.

00:01:25.142 --> 00:01:28.732
Its natural habitat is
around 3,000 feet deep,

00:01:28.732 --> 00:01:31.580
but it has often been seen shallower,

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.247
sometimes as shallow as 60 feet.

00:01:37.742 --> 00:01:39.572
You know, it's strange.

00:01:39.572 --> 00:01:41.361
Rather than swim in schools,

00:01:41.361 --> 00:01:45.462
the giant oarfish chooses a solitary life,

00:01:45.462 --> 00:01:48.030
circumnavigating the planet in an

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.262
endless search for food.

00:01:50.262 --> 00:01:52.892
I like to think it's on a grand adventure,

00:01:52.892 --> 00:01:55.030
kind of like all of us, as we—

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:55.863
- Captain.

00:01:58.361 --> 00:02:01.361
- Can't you see I'm having a moment?

00:02:07.642 --> 00:02:09.892
(laughter)

